148 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 83 0 R
/E (This project aims to build a 3D computer graphics simulation of the Solar System with visually realistic motion. The core problem is how to represent the movement of celestial bodies in a way that is both believable and computationally manageable within a real-time rendering environment.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
149 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 83 0 R
/E (Modeling the Solar System introduces several practical challenges. Planetary motion involves continuous rotation and revolution, which must be synchronized over time without introducing instability or visual artifacts. In addition, the large variation in planetary sizes and distances makes it impractical to use true physical scales directly, requiring controlled approximations that preserve the overall structure of the system.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
150 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 83 0 R
/E (Another key issue is balancing realism with performance. While physically accurate simulations rely on complex numerical models, this project focuses on visually convincing motion using simplified mathematical representations that are suitable for real-time graphics \(e.g., OpenGL\). The problem, therefore, is not full scientific accuracy, but achieving consistency, clarity, and smooth animation within technical constraints.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
